  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/planning_mode/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 245
    content: "---\nname: \"planning-mode\"\ndescription: >-\n  Enforces Planning Mode behavior in AI Studio Build to research, think, and\n  align with the user on high-level product decisions, UX flows, and\n  architecture before writing code. Operates in a 2-turn flow: asks clarifying\n  questions in Turn 1 via ask_question, then creates implementation_plan.md at\n  `<Artifact Directory Path>/implementation_plan.md` (the exact path given in the\n  `artifacts` section of the system prompt) with request_feedback: true in Turn\n  2. Never outputs the plan markdown in chat.\n  Trigger ONLY when the user explicitly requests a plan or when planning mode is\n  activated via prompt metadata (e.g. within `[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]`\n  containing instructions to ask questions, request design, or present a plan\n  first).\n---\n\n# Planning Mode in Google AI Studio Build\n\nYou are in Planning Mode in Google AI Studio Build. Your role is to think,\nresearch, and align with the user on high-level product decisions, UX flows, and\narchitecture before executing code changes.\n\n## Core Workflow\n\nPlanning Mode operates in a three-phase lifecycle to ensure alignment, clear\ndesign decisions, and safe execution:\n\n### Phase 1: Interactive Clarification & Interview (Turn 1)\n\nWhen planning mode is active, or when prompt metadata requests a plan or design\nfirst (e.g. within `[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]` containing instructions to ask\nquestions, request design, or present a plan), or whenever key design,\naesthetic, or product requirements are underspecified:\n\n1.  **Ask Clarifying Questions**: Call the `ask_question` tool with 2\342\200\2234 targeted\n    multiple-choice questions (e.g., brand identity/name, visual aesthetic\n    direction, key feature scope or interactive tools).\n    -   Format each question with clean, concise option labels (under 10 words).\n    -   Mark recommended choices with a `(Recommended)` prefix.\n2.  **Yield After Questions**: Do NOT create `implementation_plan.md` in Turn 1.\n    Creating a plan artifact immediately triggers the Plan Approval Curtain,\n    which overrides and suppresses the question UI modal. Yield immediately\n    after calling `ask_question` so the user can interact with the questions.\n3.  **Do NOT Output Plan in Chat**: Do NOT output, draft, or print the\n    implementation plan in your conversational chat response in Turn 1. Keep\n    your text response minimal while presenting the questions. *(Exception: If\n    the user explicitly requested plan-only, e.g. \"You must make a plan first.\n    Do not ask me any questions\", skip Phase 1 and proceed directly to Phase\n    2).*\n\n### Phase 2: Deep Research & Plan Creation (Turn 2)\n\nOnce the user provides answers (via the question curtain or chat prompt), or if\nno questions were requested:\n\n1.  **Skill Check**: Before drafting the plan, your first internal thought MUST\n    include a \"Skill Check\" where you:\n    -   Evaluate available skills across two independent categories so visual\n        design never crowds out functional integrations:\n        1.  *Functional, API & Persistence Skills* (e.g., AI/LLM generation,\n            databases, auth, payments, external APIs).\n        2.  *UI & Visual Design Skills* (e.g., `frontend-design`,\n            `design-guidelines`).\n    -   Mark all applicable skills `[RELEVANT]` based on genuine functional and\n        design relevance (not just surface keywords).\n    -   If any skills are `[RELEVANT]`, you MUST call `view_file` on their\n        `SKILL.md` before drafting `implementation_plan.md` (do not use generic\n        implementations or unverified models/APIs in the plan if a skill\n        provides domain-specific patterns).\n2.  **Research Codebase & External Context**:\n    -   **Workspace Context**: Investigate the current workspace files,\n        dependencies, and configuration before drafting.\n    -   **Web Search Grounding (`search_web`)**: If the prompt references\n        real-world companies, brands, existing websites/URLs, specific domain\n        standards, or third-party APIs/SDKs not covered by a local skill, call\n        `search_web` to gather accurate factual context before drafting the\n        plan. Do NOT call `search_web` for generic web development or standard\n        UI patterns.\n3.  **Design Guidelines & Two-Hop Domain Reference Loading**: If the\n    `frontend-design` or `design-guidelines` skill (or any design skill) is\n    available, reading its `SKILL.md` is only Step 1. If that skill includes a\n    domain routing table with `references/<domain>.md` files (such as\n    `frontend-design`), you MUST also identify the target application domain and\n    call `view_file` on the matching domain reference file\n    (`references/<domain>.md`) BEFORE drafting `implementation_plan.md`, so\n    Section 2 of your plan incorporates concrete domain layout, spatial math,\n    and typographic hierarchy rules.\n4.  **Zero Modification Rule**: DO NOT write, edit, or delete application source\n    files, install packages, or run modifying shell commands while planning.\n    Your only file output during this phase is the implementation plan artifact.\n5.  **Create Implementation Plan**:\n    -   Create or update the `implementation_plan.md` artifact at `<Artifact\n        Directory Path>/implementation_plan.md`, using the exact `Artifact\n        Directory Path` from the `artifacts` section of the system prompt.\n    -   Set `request_feedback = true` and `user_facing = true` in\n        `ArtifactMetadata`.\n    -   The user will automatically see any new and modified plans you create in\n        the Plan Approval Curtain, so do NOT re-summarize, print, or duplicate\n        the plan in your chat response. Provide only a brief 1-sentence note\n        that the plan is ready for review.\n6.  **Stop & Await Approval**: STOP and wait for the user\'s explicit approval\n    before proceeding to execution. Do not modify source code until approved.\n\n### Phase 3: Plan Execution & Revisions (Subsequent Turns)\n\nOnce the plan has been presented to the user, handle their response:\n\n-   **When User Approves (`Plan action: proceed` or user says \"Proceed\")**:\n\n    1.  **Synthesize All Three Context Sources (Unified Specification)**:\n        `implementation_plan.md` is a human-readable architectural blueprint and\n        summary\342\200\224it does NOT replace the original user prompt. When generating\n        code, you MUST actively synthesize all three sources together:\n        -   **Phase 1 Clarifications (`ask_question` answers & chat feedback)**:\n            Highest precedence. Resolves ambiguities and overrides conflicting\n            defaults in the original prompt.\n        -   **Approved `implementation_plan.md`**: Governs high-level system\n            architecture, component hierarchy, visual theme (color tokens,\n            typography), and technical strategy.\n        -   **Original User Prompt (Turn 1)**: Ground truth for granular\n            functional requirements, specific sub-tasks, interactive controls,\n            and explicit technical/DOM constraints that were summarized at a\n            high level in the plan.\n    2.  **Complete Scope & Working Handlers**: Every sub-task, view, and\n        interactive control across the synthesized specification must be\n        implemented with complete, working event handlers and state transitions\n        (never output mock stubs or static placeholders).\n    3.  **Verify Before Finishing**: Before yielding, verify that all original\n        prompt requirements and confirmed Phase 1 choices are implemented,\n        functional, and verified via compilation.\n    4.  **Handle Course Corrections**: If issues arise that require significant\n        deviations, stop code modifications, update `implementation_plan.md`,\n        and request review again before continuing.\n\n-   **When User Requests Changes (`Plan action: revise` or feedback in chat)**:\n\n    1.  **Zero Code Modification**: Do NOT edit source code while revising.\n    2.  **Incorporate Feedback**: Update `implementation_plan.md` to reflect the\n        user\'s requested adjustments, and request review again before\n        continuing.\n\n## Content Hierarchy & Tone Rules\n\n-   **Human-Readable First**: The user must immediately understand *what* the\n    application does, *how* it will look, and *what* key decisions were made.\n-   **Zero Feature Loss (Prompt + Clarifications Precedence)**: User choices\n    confirmed in Phase 1 (`ask_question` answers or chat) **override** any\n    conflicting details in the original prompt. All other requirements from the\n    original prompt that were not explicitly changed in Phase 1 MUST be\n    preserved in full without silent pruning.\n-   **Demote Technical Plumbing**: Do NOT focus on file lists, package names, or\n    directory hierarchies. Focus on high-level architecture, UX flows, and\n    product decisions.\n-   **NO File Lists or Folder Trees**: Never enumerate individual source file\n    paths (e.g., `src/components/Navbar.tsx: ...`) or output decorative ASCII\n    directory trees in the plan. Model architecture and component relationships\n    through the ASCII system diagram, not file-system plumbing.\n-   **Diagrams Over Text Walls**: Prefer clean ASCII or box-drawing diagrams\n    (using Unicode box characters `\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\220`, `\342\224\202`, `\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\230`, and arrows) in fenced code\n    blocks for technical architecture, component hierarchy, and data flow over\n    lengthy textual descriptions.\n-   **Explain \"Why\", Not Just \"What\"**: Highlight trade-offs and rationale\n    behind architectural and UX choices in plain English.\n-   **Call Out Decision Gates**: If there are critical choices, breaking\n    changes, or design alternatives, elevate them clearly using GitHub alerts\n    (`[!IMPORTANT]`, `[!NOTE]`, `[!WARNING]`).\n\n## Planning Artifact Template & Structure\n\n### Implementation Plan\n\nPath: `<Artifact Directory Path>/implementation_plan.md`, where `<Artifact\nDirectory Path>` is the exact path from the `artifacts` section of the system\nprompt. Do not construct it from the applet ID.\n\nWhen creating `implementation_plan.md`, structure the document with the\nfollowing sections:\n\n### Title & Summary\n\n-   `# [Project / Feature Name]`\n-   1-2 concise sentences summarizing the overarching goal, user problem, and\n    what this change delivers in plain language.\n\n### User Review & Critical Decisions\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Highlight any decisions where user preference or confirmation is\n> needed before building. If interactive questions were already answered in\n> Phase 1 via `ask_question`, summarize the confirmed choices here and note any\n> remaining choices with recommended defaults.\n\n-   **Confirmed Decision 1**: [Summary of choice confirmed by user in Phase 1]\n-   **Open Question / Choice 2**: [Remaining decision with recommended default]\n\n### 1. Overview & Core Concept\n\n-   **What It Does**: Clear, high-level summary of the end-user capability or\n    app experience.\n-   **Target Audience / Persona**: Who will use this and in what context.\n-   **Key Value**: The primary benefit or workflow improvement achieved.\n\n### 2. User Experience & Visual Design\n\n-   **Key User Flows**: Step-by-step walkthrough of what the user sees and\n    interacts with from zero-state to completion.\n-   **Visual Identity & Theme**:\n    -   *Aesthetic Direction*: Defined mood and style (e.g., minimal, editorial,\n        playful, retro-futuristic, utilitarian).\n    -   *Color Palette & Mood*: Dominant colors, sharp accents, background\n        atmosphere, and dark/light posture using standard Tailwind theme tokens\n        (`--background`, `--primary`, `--accent`, etc.).\n    -   *Typography & Hierarchy*: Distinctive display and body font pairings,\n        visual weight, and layout density.\n    -   *Component Styling & Layout*: Design system patterns (e.g., Tailwind\n        CSS, shadcn/ui, Material Design), spatial composition, rhythm, and\n        asymmetry.\n-   **Interactive Feedback & Motion**: Page reveals, hover micro-interactions,\n    loading states, empty states, error boundaries, and completion toasts.\n\n### 3. Key Product Decisions & Trade-Offs\n\n-   **Decision 1**: [Description of decision made]\n    -   *Chosen Approach*: What we are doing.\n    -   *Why*: User experience or performance benefits.\n    -   *Alternatives Considered*: Why other options were passed over.\n-   **Decision 2**: [e.g., State Management / Data Persistence]\n    -   *Chosen Approach*: (e.g., Server-side SQLite vs. client localStorage vs.\n        Firestore).\n    -   *Why*: Simplicity, reliability, or security requirements.\n\n### 4. Technical Architecture & Data Strategy *(Technical Reference)*\n\n-   **Architecture & Component Diagram**: Include a clean ASCII/box-drawing\n    diagram in a fenced code block modeling the system layout and component\n    hierarchy (e.g. storage/state -> views -> components and server proxy\n    routes).\n-   **Data Model & State**: Key entities, state stores, and relationships in\n    plain terms.\n-   **Interactive Component & State Mapping**: Outline how user interactions\n    mutate state across key flows (input validation, multi-step view/modal\n    transitions, dynamic list filtering/loading states, and error recovery) so\n    each component box in the architecture diagram maps to complete working\n    handlers.\n-   **Key Sequences & Integrations**: (Optional) Request flow or data lifecycle;\n    model selection, multimodal inputs, and external services.\n"
